  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nPrimary near term forecast issues center on fog this morning and the \npotential of an isolated shower or thunderstorm this afternoon.\n\nSfc pressure field has become weaker this morning in wake of weak \ncold front that tracked across the area over past 6 to 12 hours. \nLight winds and residual near sfc moisture have promoted some \npatchy fog development across the area. Recent rainfall along \nthe US 24 corridor yesterday should aid in fog formation and \nthe southeast expansion of the patchy fog early this morning. \nIssued an SPS earlier this morning highlighting rapidly varying \nvisibilities with patchy dense fog potential. Trends will \ncontinue to be monitored for potential of a focused dense fog \nadvisory.\n\nFor today, low level cold advection will continue. Mixed layer \ndepths should increase enough to allow for temps to be fairly \nsimilar to that of yesterday around the 80 degree mark. The initial \nmid/upper level trough axis that aided in yesterday's showers and \nstorms have shifted east of the region. Water vapor imagery early \nthis morning does depict another upper level disturbance dropping \nsoutheast across southern Minnesota/northern Iowa which will likely \nshift across the western Great Lakes during peak heating while \nanother subtle mid level trough tracks across central Lake Michigan \nvicinity early this afternoon. A secondary low level trough should \nshift southeast across southeast Lower Michigan this afternoon in \nresponse to this mid/upper level evolution. If decent heating, temps \nin lower 80s, and steep low level lapse rates do materialize as \nexpected today, some isolated shower activity is possible this \nafternoon/early evening particularly across NW Indiana/NE Illinois \nin closer proximity to the stronger mid level trough and where some \nlake reinforced boundary is possible. Confidence remains low in \ncoverage, and for this issuance have kept PoPs in the 10-15 percent \nrange. Skies should clear this evening, with some potential of \nshallow patchy fog once again early Friday morning, but currently \nnot expecting this to be quite as prevalent as this morning.\n\nMid level ridging across the Corn Belt will slowly approach the \nwestern Great Lakes late Friday/early Saturday, and approach of this \nfeature will result in weak low level thermal advections \nThursday night into early Saturday. A slowly modifying low level\nthermal trough will allow for a continuation of near seasonable\ntemperatures Friday into Saturday.\n\nBroad upper ridging is then expected to overspread mid/upper MS \nValley late weekend as a stronger upper trough across Pacific NW \nlifts into south central Canada by Monday. Veering low level flow \nwill allow for some better moisture transport into the area late \nSunday into early Monday. Precip chances remain an item of very low \nconfidence however given uncertainty as to how efficient these \nnorthern latitude short waves will dampen the upper ridge, and also \nthe potential of very warm mid level thermal profiles acting to \ncreate a good deal of CIN for near sfc/sfc based parcels. It is \npossible some convection for elevated parcels develops late Sunday \ninto Monday but greater confidence in higher PoPs may remain to the \nnorth across the northern/eastern Great Lakes where warm/moist \nadvective forcing should be maximized. Broadbrushed slight \nchance/low chance PoPs have been maintained through much of the \nMon-Wed period with a potential of moderate to strong \ninstability and an instability gradient somewhere across the \nregion. However, confidence in day to day PoPs remains quite low\ngiven greater uncertainty in longwave ridge amplitude for the \nlatter periods. An extended period of above normal temperatures \nremains in item of higher confidence for much of next week.",
